QSapecNG CHANGELOG
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

 * 3.0.0 (work in progress)
    o Ported to Qt 6 (6.2 or newer), Qwt 6.2/6.3 and C++17; builds with
      current GCC, Clang, MSVC and MinGW, CMake 3.16 - 4.x, Boost up to 1.90
    o Modern CMake build (AUTOMOC/AUTORCC, imported targets); Windows builds
      copy Qwt and run windeployqt automatically
    o Property browser removed: parameters are edited in a dialog
      (double-click, or Edit > Properties...)
    o New look: Fusion style where the platform style is dated, scalable
      line icons, HiDPI symbol icons, tabbed documents, sans-serif labels,
      restyled plots; system, light or dark theme
    o Double-click a component to edit its parameters in a dialog (name,
      value with unit, symbolic flag; both coils of a mutual inductance);
      undoable
    o R rotates the selected items, or the item being placed
    o Wire drawing as in KiCad / Eagle: W starts the tool; each click adds
      a corner and drawing continues until it ends on a pin or a wire, or
      on a double-click; the bend follows the first move (right-click or /
      flips it); Esc drops the unfinished segment, a second Esc leaves
    o Resistor symbol: IEC / DIN (rectangle, default) or ANSI
      (zigzag)
    o Result panel (tabbed with Messages) replaces the result popup: real
      fractions, Result / Raw / Digit sections with Copy links, and a notice
      when the circuit changed since it was solved
    o Component labels are centred beside the symbol (also when rotated or
      mirrored) and stay centred when renamed; a dragged label keeps its
      place. Older files get the automatic placement
    o Net numbers shown after Resolve leave out the ground net (0)
    o Values can be typed with prefixes: 4.7k, 4k7, 4R7, 100n, 2.2u or
      2.2µ, 1meg, also with the unit (10 kΩ, 100nF); stored as plain numbers
    o Result panel: Copy LaTeX (\frac{...}{...}) next to each Copy
    o File > Export Schematic (Ctrl+E): SVG, PNG (~300 dpi) or PDF, black on
      white without the grid; printing also leaves out grid and selection
    o Example circuits: RC low-pass, RLC band-pass, inverting amplifier,
      Sallen-Key low-pass; installed with the program and listed in
      File > Open Example
    o Junction dots only where three or more wires or pins meet; plain
      bends and wire ends on pins have none
    o Picking a component with no schematic open starts a new one
    o The splash screen can be turned off; it stays up about 2.5 s
    o A bent wire acts as one object: clicking a segment selects the whole
      run up to junctions and pins (move, delete), and a wire drawn in one
      go is one undo step. "Wire cross" removed (W draws all wires)
    o Box selection as in CAD: dragging right selects what is entirely
      inside (blue box), dragging left also what the box touches (green)
    o Results simplified for reading: a numeric denominator is divided out
      ((-V1) / 0.1 -> -10 V1), minus signs instead of "( - x )",
      parentheses only around a sum multiplied by s
    o The status bar progress indicator only appears while solving
    o Results show the input factored out: -(R2 / R1) V1, H(s) V1
    o Editor zoom with the mouse wheel around the pointer (Shift / Ctrl +
      wheel scroll), middle-button drag pans; the canvas extends around
      the drawing and the grid thins out when zoomed out
    o The Out marker is a tag ("Out") pointing at its terminal
    o Plot markers: click a plot to place a marker (a dashed line with
      frequency, magnitude and phase), click the line to remove it. The
      Markers panel beside each plot lists them: edit a frequency (10k,
      2.5MEG), Add, Remove, and move the selected one left / right by a
      step in Hz (prefixes allowed). Values come from the transfer
      function, markers stay when the circuit is solved again; Plot >
      Markers Panel (Ctrl+M), Plot > Clear Markers
    o Plots in a window of their own (Plots, one tab per plot) instead of
      the Editor / Workplane tabs: Run > New Plot (F9) asks for the kind,
      frequency range and axes; several plots at once, with the Plot menu
      and a toolbar in that window. Plots follow every Resolve (F8);
      Plot > Plot Settings changes the
      current one, Window > Plots Window (Ctrl+Shift+P) brings it back;
      size and place are remembered
    o Plot Settings is a panel beside the plots (Plot > Plot Settings):
      it shows the current plot's settings and applies every change at
      once; Run > New Plot keeps its dialog
    o Markers panel: closed until the first marker is placed; the Peak
      buttons move the selected marker to the previous / next peak of the
      magnitude; -3 dB places
      a marker where the magnitude is 3 dB below the selected one and makes
      that the reference (bandwidth in the difference columns)
    o Pole-zero map: the same scale on both axes, whatever the window's
      shape
    o Plot > Antialiasing (and Configure): smooth curves on or off (kept);
      the grid and the marker lines stay sharp either way, and the setting
      reaches every curve of a plot (those of another plot kind or unit
      kept the old one until shown). Curves are drawn at their exact
      positions (Qwt rounded every point to a whole pixel, so even smooth
      curves came out in steps)
    o The Markers panel is a side panel of the Plots window, like Plot
      Settings, with its own close button; it shows the markers of the
      plot in front. Right-click on a plot: Add Marker Here (at the
      frequency clicked) and Zoom to Fit (Reset View renamed; Home). The
      side panels no longer float off as windows of their own, and Plot
      Settings is never narrower than its form (no horizontal scroll bar)
    o Differential markers: Reference makes the selected marker the
      reference; the others show their difference from it in magnitude
      and phase (in the panel and on the plot) and in frequency
    o Plot axes use prefixes (1k, 10M); pole and zero labels read
      -5k + j5k
    o Out tag 10 % lower
    o Real resistors, inductors and capacitors: untick "Ideal" in the
      parameters to add parasitics - R: series L and parallel C; L: winding
      resistance and parallel C; C: ESR, ESL and leakage resistance (0 =
      none). The part itself stays symbolic, the parasitics are numeric
    o Context menu: Rotate (R), Flip Horizontally (H), Flip Vertically
      (V), clipboard, order, and a Place submenu with every part; new
      hotkeys G (ground), P (port), O (Out), T (label)
    o A part being placed hangs on the pointer by its pin (one-pin parts),
      the middle between its pins (two pins) or its centre (more), always
      on a grid point
    o Click a pin or a wire end to start a wire there (a drag still moves
      the part)
    o Text labels: T hangs a label on the pointer and asks for its text
      (several lines) when it is placed; a click selects it, a drag moves
      it on the grid, a double-click edits it
    o Component labels sit closer to their parts; a dragged label snaps
      back to its centred place when brought near it; right-click > Reset
      Label Position
    o Grid: dots or lines, faint / medium / strong
    o A wire ending on the middle of another splits it there, so a click
      selects wires only up to the junction (a click no longer glues
      touching wires together)
    o Fixed: a crash when a part was picked with its hotkey (T, G, P, O)
      while the pointer was over the schematic
    o Pasted parts follow the pointer until a click places them (right
      click rotates, Esc takes the paste back) and get names of their own
      (a copy of R2 becomes R3; duplicate names merged in the result)
    o Flip Horizontally / Vertically refer to the screen also for rotated
      parts (they flipped about the part's own axis)
    o Moving parts tidies the wires: pieces lying on top of each other are
      merged, and a wire end left connected to nothing is trimmed back, so
      moving a part away and back leaves no stubs or stray junction dots
    o Mutual inductance: Lp and Ls are small upright captions beside the
      coils instead of outlines across them
    o Fixed: Esc did not cancel a part picked in the Components list (the
      list kept the keyboard)
    o Fixed: markers kept the colours of the theme they were made in
    o Fixed: the Markers panel squeezed the Phase column after Clear Markers
    o Fixed: the Font page icon vanished when selected in the Configure
      dialog
    o Fixed: the Out tag looked grey (edges and caption between pixels)
    o Components list: one click picks the part to place (dragging still
      works); a click below the list goes back to the plain pointer; the
      list shows the part being placed
    o Moving parts keeps them connected: wires ending on a moved pin
      stretch along (straight, or with a bend so the pin is entered as
      before), and a part touching a moved pin directly gets a new wire;
      a mouse drag or an arrow key is one undo step
    o Net numbers after Resolve only for nets joining two or more pins
      (the Out tag counts), numbered 1, 2, 3 ... without gaps, placed on a
      wire corner beside the wires instead of on a lead
    o Resolve leaves out parts connected to nothing (they made the whole
      result 0) and says so in Messages
    o Dragged labels snap to the grid
    o Fit to Window centres the drawing both ways
    o Op-amp: GBW defaults to 1 MHz; its label reads G= for the gain
    o Points per decade up to 10000
    o Plots: Frequency Response, Phase Response, Bode Plot (both on
      one graph, phase on the right axis) and Poles & Zeros (centred on
      the origin), with units on the axes; options for magnitude in dB or
      linear, phase in degrees or radians, frequency in Hz or rad/s, and
      log or linear axes (kept between sessions). The separate Gain and
      Loss plots, the Data box and the result tooltip are gone; values
      come from the schematic
    o Plots: mouse wheel zooms around the pointer (Shift: frequency only,
      Ctrl: vertical only), middle button pans, Plot > Zoom to Fit
      (Home); Plot > Vertical Range sets a fixed or automatic range,
      kept per quantity (dB, linear, degrees, radians; both Bode axes)
    o Plot > Frequency Range: automatic (a decade beyond the poles
      and zeros, the default) or start/stop as typed (10k, 1MEG), with
      log-spaced points per decade instead of a fixed step
    o Status bar: what is going on and what the active tool expects, the
      number of parts and wires, zoom and pointer position
    o Op-amps: "Ideal" (as before) or a real one with an open-loop gain
      (100dB, 200V/mV, 1e5V/V or a number; can stay symbolic) and an
      optional gain-bandwidth product (single-pole model)
    o Gain values accept dB, V/mV and V/V
    o Voltage Source (1-pin): a source from the ground to its one pin,
      drawn as a lead ending in a small circle (saved as a voltage source plus
      its own type, so older versions open it as a two-pin source)
    o Repository cleanup: unused bitmap icons and symbols (no longer built
      into the program), old SapecNG images, macOS leftovers, the 2014 TODO
    o Component labels: name only (default) or name and value,
      e.g. "C1 / 1 µF"
    o Side panels start closed except Components and Messages (Window >
      Show); a saved layout from an earlier version is replaced once
    o Pole-zero map always uses linear axes (poles were invisible with the
      default logarithmic x axis)
    o Fixed: new parts could repeat a name already in the schematic (e.g. a
      second R1 after opening a file)
    o Fixed: a rotated resistor or conductance moved off its wires when the
      symbol style (ANSI / DIN) differed from the one it was drawn with
    o Fixed: after switching from dark to light (or back) with no
      schematic open, the editor background and the component icons kept
      the old colours
    o Fixed: Poles & Zeros crashed for a constant transfer function (e.g. an
      inverting amplifier); it now says there are none. Roots at s = 0 are
      no longer counted twice
    o Fixed: an op-amp's value and symbolic flag were ignored when a file
      was read
    o Fixed: segmentation fault on Resolve when terms cancel out in the
      transfer function (issue #6): the solver advanced an erased iterator
    o Fixed: saving without a known extension wrote an empty file and
      reported success (issue #4, PR #7)
    o Fixed: a wire ending in the middle of another wire (T-junction) looked
      connected but was not (issue #5); it now connects, with a junction
      dot, while wires that merely cross stay unconnected
    o Fixed: dropping a wire onto a component treated the component as a
      wire (undefined behaviour while looking for wires to merge)
    o Fixed: delete vs delete[] mismatches in the solver
    o Fixed: mutual inductance read its coil values as whole numbers
      (0.001 H became 0 H, 1.5 H became 1 H), so every circuit with one
      had a wrong result
    o Fixed: a netlist whose node numbers have a gap (an unused number,
      e.g. a net with only a loose wire, or a hand-written netlist) was
      solved as 0; nodes are now numbered internally
    o Circuits without a unique solution (a loop of voltage sources,
      current sources in series, a floating group, an op-amp without
      feedback) are reported as such, with the usual causes, instead of a
      result of 0 or 1/0; no plot opens for them
    o Plots use the numeric result directly (as Digit in the Result panel)
      instead of evaluating the symbolic one with values collected again
      from the parts, where a name not found counted as 0
    o Saving numbers the nodes in the file even when the circuit was never
      solved (they were all saved as 0, so the file was no usable
      netlist); the examples are saved again
    o Faster solving of larger circuits: equal terms are found through an
      index instead of a linear search (25 parts: 65 s down to 9 s)
    o Solver regression tests (cmake -DQSAPECNG_BUILD_TESTS=ON, ctest),
      among them an independent numeric solver (modified nodal analysis)
      that checks the engine on random circuits with every component
      type, the poles and zeros, and the plotted frequency response
    o Settings > Configure QSapecNG gathers the options: General (splash
      screen, log), Appearance (theme, resistor symbol, component labels,
      grid style and strength, plot antialiasing) and Font; OK, Apply
      (every page) and Close
    o Voltmeter and ammeter: right-click > Reverse Polarity swaps the + / -
      ends (the arrow for an ammeter) and so the sign of the result; undoable
      and saved
    o Result panel: the Result, Raw and Digit sections sit side by side
      when they fit the panel's width
    o Result panel: a result too long for the panel's width is shown on
      one line, cut short with "+ ...", with an Expand link to show it all
      (Collapse to go back); kept per output when solving again
    o README: Quick start instead of the old Usage and Translations, and a
      What's new in 3.0 section
    o Several outputs: every Out marker, voltmeter and ammeter is solved,
      each with a name (Out1, Out2, VM1, AM1 ... kept in the file); the
      Result panel has a tab per output, and a plot shows the output chosen
      in New Plot or in the Plot Settings panel (and in its tab title).
      Placing a second output no longer dims the first. Older versions
      read the first Out marker of such a file; .crc netlists keep one
    o The Out tag grows with its name; double-click an Out marker or a
      meter (or Properties) to rename it - Vout, Vbe ... - for the sheet,
      the Result tabs and the plots; undoable, two outputs cannot share a
      name
    o Fixed: after a Resolve, Undo and Redo stayed disabled until another
      schematic was picked, and the progress bar stayed in the status bar
    o Fixed: a meter's output could be lost when the netlist numbered a
      node after it (the solver moved the meter's internal node but kept
      the output on the old one)
    o Run > Plots (F9) brings up the plots of the schematic, or opens a
      Bode plot when it has none (solving first if need be); New Plot
      (Ctrl+F9, Plot menu and the plot window) asks for the kind
    o Resolve no longer opens a plot by itself: the Plots window stays
      shut until asked for (F9), and the status bar points to it. A file
      naming a kind of plot (plot.kind, as some examples) still opens it
      with its first result
    o Automatic vertical range of the plots: one major step of margin
      above and below the curves
    o Grid dots are stronger at medium and strong strength
    o Flip Vertically has an icon; the Flip Horizontally icon now shows the
      vertical axis it flips about
    o The Label tool places one label, then the pointer is back to normal;
      Ctrl+Enter finishes the label's text
    o Right-click menu: options that do nothing for the selection are left
      out (Reverse Polarity without a meter, Reset Label Position without a
      part, Properties for parts without parameters)
    o Labels beside a part (sources, meters) keep a little more distance
    o Save As has Ctrl+Shift+S on desktops without a standard key for it
    o Bring to front / Send to back removed: the stacking order of items
      changes nothing in a schematic
    o File > Export SPICE Netlist: the circuit as a .cir file for ngspice,
      LTspice and the like, with an AC sweep (the plots' range when set by
      hand) and a .print line per output. Conductances become resistors,
      an ideal op-amp a VCVS with a gain of 1e8, a real one its gain and
      pole, the ideal transformer a VCVS and a CCCS, the mutual inductance
      two coils and a K line, parasitics their own parts. A test checks
      the export with ngspice on random circuits when ngspice is installed
    o Nullor: a nullator (one circle: no voltage, no current) and a
      norator (two circles: any voltage and current) as one part with four
      free pins - the ideal op-amp without its ground, for pathological-
      element models. Saved as an ideal op-amp of its own type: older
      versions open it as an op-amp
    o Three-Winding Transformer: a primary and two secondaries on one core
      (dots mark where each winding starts), ideal (turns ratios N2/N1 and
      N3/N1) or with the primary inductance and a coupling k between every
      two windings; the secondaries in series make a centre-tapped one.
      Solved exactly as leakage inductances (1 - k) L, a magnetizing
      inductance k Lp and ideal transformers, which appear by name in the
      Raw result. Older versions skip the part
    o Solver tests: a transformer with three windings in the random
      circuits, checked against its own inductance-matrix model
    o File > Open Netlist reads a .crc netlist and draws it: the parts in
      a row, one bus per node, ground symbols and Out markers; saving
      writes the netlist back. Part names may be longer than a letter and
      digits (Rload), and ideal transformers (n) are read
    o The voltmeter and the ammeter are in the Components list (no longer
      on the toolbar)
    o Plots: the kind of plot is picked with four buttons (magnitude,
      phase, Bode, poles and zeros); New Plot from the Plots window keeps
      that window in front; the automatic vertical range fits the curves
      (a resonance peak is no longer cut off)
    o Messages and Result have Clear in their right-click menu
    o A wire started by clicking a pin or a junction ends the wire tool
      when it is finished
    o Mutual inductance: the Lp and Ls tags sit further from the windings
    o Smith chart, a fifth kind of plot: the output read as an impedance
      (a 1 A current source driving the port, the Out marker on it) is
      drawn as its reflection coefficient against Z0 (50 ohm by default),
      with labelled resistance and reactance circles; optional admittance
      circles (dashed) show where a shunt part moves the point. A click on
      the curve places a marker with Z, the reflection coefficient and the
      VSWR (the labels kept apart and inside the chart). An impedance that
      does not change with frequency (a resistor) shows as a dot
    o Plots window: Plot Settings and Markers are tabs on the right,
      always there (the toolbar buttons for them are gone; the Plot menu
      brings either tab to the front, and the first marker brings up
      Markers); the window is made tall enough for the settings (no
      scroll bar) and wide enough for a well-proportioned plot, within the
      screen; closing the last plot leaves the window open
    o New Plot dialog: fits its contents when the kind of plot changes
    o The pointer turns into the aiming cross over a pin or a wire end,
      where a click starts a wire
    o 16 examples (File > Open Example, now a scrolling list): besides the
      basics, an RF chain for 70 cm around the Semtech SX1255 (receive
      match, transmit balun, 5 W PA pi network, harmonic low-pass with
      real parts), a 10 MHz crystal model, EU FM pre-emphasis, a bridge
      with meters, and a through-hole dummy load with its parasitics
    o A schematic file can carry the sweep and kind of plot it opens with
      (plot.fstart, plot.fstop, plot.points, plot.kind, plot.z0)
    o Smith chart markers: Z, the reflection coefficient and the VSWR or,
      by a tick in Plot Settings, the return loss
    o Points per decade up to 1 000 000 for narrow sweeps (at most about
      100 000 points are computed)
    o Meters: the V and A and the + and - marks stay upright when the
      meter is turned
    o Markers: several can be selected in the table (Ctrl / Shift); the
      selected ones are drawn bold in the plot; a click on the empty part
      of the table clears the selection
    o Voltage sources can have an internal resistance (untick Ideal in
      their dialog; shown under the value, Rs = 50 Ω), and an Out marker
      a load (a resistor to the ground, shown on its tag): neither needs
      drawing. With both, the magnitude plots show the transducer gain (load power over the power the
      source can give, 0 dB = lossless), assuming a load equal to the
      source resistance when the Out marker has none; also in SPICE export
    o Editing after a Resolve is quick again: every edit laid out the whole
      result (long ones took seconds); now only an "out of date" strip
      appears above it
    o Undo no longer takes back the loading of a file (it emptied the
      schematic, and Redo brought the parts back renamed with default
      values)
    o Plot Settings: the vertical ranges (automatic or from - to) moved in
      from a separate dialog; Resolve (F8) in the Plots window solves the
      plot's own schematic; the sweep is no longer carried over to other
      plots
    o Markers: names (M1, M2 ...) in the first column; Peak also finds
      dips (notches, series resonances)
    o Menus: the Plot menu lives in the Plots window (New Plot is in Run);
      Window has no Tile / Cascade / Next / Previous (tabs); Zoom Normal
      is Actual Size (100 %), centred; Output (O) named in full
    o 14 net colours plus a grey reference (ground) net; a softer
      selection colour in the dark theme; the example list follows a
      theme switch; Esc on the Font page no longer empties it
    o No plot opens by itself for a result that does not depend on
      frequency (resistors and sources only)
    o The Plots window opens at a moderate size (settings without a
      scroll bar, the plot 1.25 times as wide as high, at most 80 % of
      the screen) and later only grows when the settings need it
    o A marker placed by a click keeps the frequency to the resolution of
      a pixel (it was rounded to 3 digits: on a narrow sweep every click
      gave the same 10 MHz); marker frequencies show all their digits
    o Smith chart: while the mouse button is held, a dotted ghost marker
      with its frequency follows the curve; releasing places the marker
      there (as the vertical line does on the other plots)
    o The frequency axis spans the sweep exactly (a narrow sweep was
      widened to whole decades, the curve squeezed out of sight); a
      vertical range set by hand belongs to its plot only
    o Frequency axis labels carry enough digits to tell a narrow sweep's
      ticks apart (10.005M, 10.01M, 10.015M)
    o Parts cannot be placed, moved, pasted or dropped on top of one
      another (meeting at the pins is fine); the status bar says why
    o A part with one pin (Out, ground, port, one-pin source) turns around
      its pin and stays on its wire; the Out tag's caption stays inside
      the tag, read upwards when the tag stands on end
    o A wire is started by a click only on a free pin or on a wire end or
      junction; a click on a connected pin selects the part
    o New application icon, sharp at every size (16 and 22 pixels drawn
      apart); the icon files are made from SVG by packaging/make-icons.py
    o The splash screen is drawn by the program: icon, name and version
      on a light card, readable on any desktop
    o Light theme on a dark desktop: the light palette is complete (it
      took Fusion's, which follows the system's dark scheme since Qt 6.5);
      with Qt 6.8 or newer, window frames and native dialogs follow the
      chosen theme too
    o Results: a sum times the input is shown in parentheses,
      (L1 s + R1) I1, as is a negated sum (it lost them when the
      denominator was 1)
    o Resolve shows its progress in percent (status bar and progress bar)
      and can be stopped: Run > Stop (Shift+F8), also in the Plots window;
      quitting or closing a schematic while it solves stops the solver
      instead of waiting for it
    o The solver uses several processor cores: outputs (Out markers,
      voltmeters, ammeters) are solved at the same time, and a single
      output is split among the cores too. Settings > General > Solver
      sets the maximum number of cores used in all. The result does not
      depend on the number of cores
    o Much faster solver: the common spanning trees are searched on plain
      arrays with union-find cycle tests (the same Minty-Read-Tarjan
      choices, in the same order), the sign of a tree takes one walk over
      it instead of an elimination, and each tree's terms are added up as
      it is found instead of keeping every tree in memory. A 7-section
      LC ladder with real L and C (32 nodes, about 4 billion trees) goes
      from about 3,300 to 220,000 trees per second on one core and needs
      some 50 MB instead of hundreds of GB; small circuits solve 10 - 30
      times faster
    o Coefficients are summed exactly (rounded once), so they no longer
      depend on the order the terms are found in. The search no longer
      produces sets that are not trees (they added nothing, but could
      move a term's place): in some results the terms come in a slightly
      different order, the values are the same
    o New tests compare the solver with the previous one term by term on
      the examples and on 600 random circuits
    o Resolve gives the numeric result (Digit) and the plots at once,
      whatever the size of the circuit: the transfer function is worked
      out as a determinant (exactly, modulo many primes), without listing
      the trees. A 7-section LC ladder with real L and C: about a second,
      where the symbolic result takes hours
    o The symbolic result (Result, Raw) of a big circuit is then worked out
      in the background, with the schematic free to edit (an edit stops
      it). Settings > General > Solver > Symbolic result: Always, Ask when
      it takes long (the default: asks when it would take more than about
      10 s, with the estimated time), or Never (numeric
      only). A small circuit gets it at once, as before. The Result panel
      says what is going on ("Calculating... 3h 50min left.") and offers
      to compute it when skipped or stopped
    o The time the symbolic result takes is estimated before it starts:
      its work is counted (small circuits) or sampled within a few per cent
      (random paths down the search), at the speed of the last symbolic
      solve on the computer
    o The progress bar is 1.5 times as wide and shows the time left of the
      symbolic result (from the terms found so far). Times are shown the
      same way everywhere: 3h 50min, 50min, 40s
    o Plots of high-order transfer functions: a term with a high power of
      s overflowed (infinity, then NaN), which drew nonsense and froze the
      window; the terms are now scaled before they are added
    o Big circuits whose coefficients span more than 300 decades (an LC
      ladder bridged by capacitors: 1 to 1e320): some were stored as
      infinities, and opening the plot froze the program (the root finder
      for the frequency range never returned). The numerator and the
      denominator are now scaled together to keep every coefficient a
      finite number (the transfer function is the same), and made monic
      only when that fits
    o Poles and zeros: the polynomial is scaled to roots of like size
      before the root finder (rpoly), which gives up instead of looping
      when it cannot cope; then the Aberth-Ehrlich method finds them
      (roots spread over many decades)
    o Markers: "< Peak" and "Peak >" from a marker already on a peak or a
      dip stayed there; they move on to the next one
    o Smith chart: offered only when the output is an impedance (a 1 A
      current source drives the circuit, no voltage source does): greyed
      out otherwise, in New Plot and in Plot Settings, saying why. A Smith
      chart of such a circuit (from a file) says it in its title. It drew
      the voltage ratio as an impedance: curves off the chart
    o Resolve no longer leaves the Plots window behind the main window:
      pressed there, it stays in front (also with the question about the
      symbolic result, which now opens over it); pressed in the main
      window, the Plots window comes back over it once the result is in
    o A crash (heap corruption) at exit with schematics open: closing them
      reached the main window after its parts were freed. A schematic
      still solving in the background is stopped when it is closed
    o The symbolic result takes about a sixth of the memory, and less
      time: a term's component names are kept once for the whole program
      (a term holds pointers to them, shared by its copies), Result shares
      Raw's terms when every component is symbolic, and the solver's
      tables are smaller (exact sums without the heap, compact keys). An
      RC ladder of 29 parts (514,229 terms): 1.3 GB and 8.6 s down to
      0.2 GB and 3.4 s on one core; 33 parts (3.5 million terms): 1.4 GB,
      where it ran out of memory before
    o A symbolic result too large for the computer's memory (half of it)
      is stopped, with a message in the Result panel; the numeric result
      and the plots are complete. Before, the program could be killed by
      the system, losing unsaved schematics
    o Result panel: a formula of thousands of terms or more shows its first
      200; Copy and Copy LaTeX give all of it.
      Laying out millions of terms took minutes and gigabytes
    o Resolve after changing only values (or which parts are symbolic):
      the symbolic result comes at once, made from the terms of the last
      one instead of listing the trees again, and without the question
      about the time it takes. It takes time by the size of the result,
      not by the number of trees: an RC ladder of 33 parts, 2 s instead
      of 26 s. A changed circuit, or a new value of a part's internals
      (ESR, GBW...), is solved anew
    o Plots window: the size it had last time is kept. It no longer grows
      to most of the screen when the Markers tab is in front
    o Components: a column of tool buttons on the left edge, as in
      Inkscape; the button of the part being placed stays pressed, and
      the names are in the tooltips. It replaces the Components list;
      the window layout starts afresh once. Docked at the top or bottom,
      the buttons are the size of the other toolbars'

